&lt;p&gt;When engineering teams estimate the gross margin of a new AI SaaS feature, they almost always calculate the cost of a &lt;strong&gt;single isolated API call&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For example, if you use &lt;strong&gt;Claude 3.7 Sonnet&lt;/strong&gt; (&lt;code&gt;$3.00 / 1M input&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;$15.00 / 1M output&lt;/code&gt;) and assume an average request consists of a &lt;code&gt;2,500-token&lt;/code&gt; system + RAG prompt and a &lt;code&gt;500-token&lt;/code&gt; response:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Single Call Cost = (2,500 × $0.000003) + (500 × $0.000015) = $0.015 (1.5 cents)
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;At &lt;code&gt;1.5 cents&lt;/code&gt; per request, a &lt;strong&gt;$29/month Pro subscription&lt;/strong&gt; looks like it can comfortably support nearly 1,500 requests per user while maintaining software-grade gross margins.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In production, that math fails catastrophically.&lt;/strong&gt; Here is why—and how to architect around it.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  1. The Quadratic Reality of Multi-Turn Agentic Sessions
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;LLMs are stateless. In a 20-turn coding assistant, legal document analyzer, or customer support copilot, every time the user sends message $k$, your backend re-transmits the static system prompt, tool schemas, retrieved RAG chunks, &lt;strong&gt;plus all previous $k-1$ user and assistant turns&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$S$ = Static system prompt + tool definitions (&lt;code&gt;3,500 tokens&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$R$ = Retrieved RAG context per turn (&lt;code&gt;2,000 tokens&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$U$ = Average user prompt (&lt;code&gt;300 tokens&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;$A$ = Average assistant output (&lt;code&gt;600 tokens&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;On Turn $k$ (for $k = 1 \dots N$), the input token count $I_k$ is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;$$I_k = S + R + (k - 1)(U + A)$$&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Across a &lt;strong&gt;$N = 20$ turn session&lt;/strong&gt;, total cumulative input tokens billed by the provider is not &lt;code&gt;5,800 tokens&lt;/code&gt;—it is:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;$$\sum_{k=1}^{20} I_k = 20(3,500 + 2,000) + (900) \times \frac{19 \times 20}{2} = 110,000 + 171,000 = 281,000 \text{ input tokens}$$&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Add in multi-step tool calls (where an agent loops 3 times per user message), and a single 20-turn session routinely crosses &lt;strong&gt;650,000 input tokens and 35,000 output tokens&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;




